<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Luo peoples</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Luo are a group of Nilotic peoples who spread from the region of the southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a> into present-day Uganda, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kenya">Kenya</a>, Tanzania and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/democratic-republic-of-the-congo">Democratic Republic of the Congo</a> from around the fifteenth century. They include the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acholi">Acholi</a>, Alur, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lango">Lango</a> and Kenya Luo. According to tradition, Luo-speaking migrants founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babito-dynasty">Babito dynasty</a> that took power in Bunyoro-Kitara.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/luo-peoples.jpg" alt="Luo peoples" width="210" height="210" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Giovannigobbin · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:LuoLocation.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Luo, also spelled Lwo, speak languages of the western branch of the Nilotic family; the Nilotes had separated from the other members of the East Sudanic family by about the 3rd millennium BCE. Linguists distinguish a Northern Luo group, spoken mostly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-sudan">South Sudan</a> by the Shilluk, Anuak, Pari and others, from a Southern group that includes Dholuo. The dispersion from a presumed core region in South Sudan is thought to have been set off by the turmoil of the Muslim conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudan">Sudan</a>.</p><p>A widespread oral tradition names Nyikango, dated to about the mid-15th century, as the founder of the Shilluk nation. After a quarrel with his brother he moved north along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nile">Nile</a> and established a feudal society, and the Pari descend from the group that rejected him.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the 1500s a small group known as the Biito-Luo, or Paluo, led by Labongo, settled among <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bantu-peoples">Bantu-speaking</a> peoples in Bunyoro and founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babiito-dynasty">Babiito dynasty</a> in place of the Bachwezi dynasty of Kitara. Legend makes <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isingoma-mpuga-rukidi">Isingoma Mpuga Rukidi</a>, the first Babiito king, the twin of Kato Kimera, first king of Buganda. These Luo were absorbed into Bantu society and lost their language. The Padhola under their chief Adhola settled in a thickly forested part of Budama in eastern Uganda, an isolation that kept their language alive, and in the 18th century other Luo speakers formed the Alur, Jonam and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acholi">Acholi</a>.</p><p>Between about 1500 and 1800 further groups reached the shores of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-victoria">Lake Victoria</a> in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kenya">Kenya</a> and Tanzania; the Joluo say the warrior chief Ramogi Ajwang led them there. The Jo k'Ajok moved into the Kavirondo Gulf, the Jo k'Owiny settled near Ramogi hill, where their ruins survive at Bungu Owiny near Lake Kanyaboli, and around 1750 the Jo Kager under Ochieng Waljak Ger drove Bantu groups out of Ugenya.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The Joluo are traditionally fishermen, and boys aged 13 to 18 took part in wrestling within their age sets. Their population in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kenya">Kenya</a> was estimated at 6.1 million in 2017, and in Tanzania at 1,980,000 in 2010. Dholuo is mutually intelligible, to varying degrees, with the languages of the Alur, Acoli and Padhola, and most of the Bantu Suba of Kenya now speak it as a first language. The Anuak live in villages along the rivers of south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> and across the border in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-sudan">South Sudan</a>, on flat grassland that floods in the rainy season.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Luo peoples” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The first Mubito king is remembered as Isingoma Mpuga Rukidi, who adopted the regalia and rituals of his predecessors and presented his line as their rightful heir; historians accept a <b>Luo</b> migration and a change of dynasty but regard the details and the date as uncertain.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-bunyoro">Kingdom of Bunyoro · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
